<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayak</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as qajaq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kayak is a small, narrow boat with an enclosed deck, propelled by a long double-bladed paddle; its name comes from the Inuktitut word qajaq. It was first used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleut">Aleut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> and Yupik hunters, and possibly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>, in the subarctic, and is believed to be at least 4,000 years old. The first kayaks were made of stitched seal or other animal skins stretched over a frame of wood or whalebone, each built by the man who used it to fit his own body. Hunters used them for seals above all, and in some areas for whales and caribou; skin-on-frame kayaks are still used for hunting by Inuit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayak.jpg" alt="Kayak" width="330" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ville Miettinen · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greenland_kayak_seal_hunter_2006.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The qajaq, a one-person sealskin boat that could be righted even when completely overturned, was copied by others as the <b>kayak</b>, while the larger open umiaq carried people, goods and dogs.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kayaks</b> and umiaks, large boats of skin on a frame, first appear in the archaeological record in the Old Bering Sea stage, and the umiak became central to bowhead whaling.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thule-people">Thule people · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
